{"reference":"1 Kings 17","book":"1 Kings","chapter":17,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Elijah and prayer","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"A later interpretation presents Elijah as fully human like his audience, while associating his prayer with drought and renewed rain. That connection encourages prayer, but it does not turn weather or material provision into a technique that can be guaranteed.","verse_refs":["1 Kings 17:1-24"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_kings_017_2e57d7664106778c43e46cb6"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Ahab in a wider setting","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The Kurkh Monolith names Ahab in a coalition at Qarqar, an event not narrated in 1 Kings. It independently places an Israelite king within ninth-century regional politics, though it does not confirm every detail or theological claim in the book.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_kings_017_959db899f19d31b5cc1ffbeb"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"inference"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}